## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This problem explores non-overlapping palindromic substring selection using a two-dimensional DP that tracks prefix length and the number of selected substrings. It tests palindrome precomputation, transition logic, and solution reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Palindrome table**: pal[l][r] indicates whether s[l..r] is a palindrome
- **DP state**: dp[i][t] represents the maximum total length obtainable from prefix s[0..i] using exactly t substrings
- **Transition**: Either skip the current position or end a palindrome at i (starting at some l)
- **Reconstruction**: Use parent pointers to backtrack and retrieve the selected substrings

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalindromes(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pal[i][i] = true;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len - 1 < n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      if (s[l] === s[r] && (len === 2 || pal[l+1][r-1])) {
        pal[l][r] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1]?.[t] ?? 0;
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        if (pal[l][i]) {
          const prev = l > 0 ? dp[l-1][t-1] : 0;
          const val = prev + (i - l + 1);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            parent[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t-1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (i >= 0 && t > 0) {
    const choice = parent[i][t];
    if (!choice.skip) {
      result.unshift([choice.l, i]);
      t = choice.t;
      i = choice.l - 1;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxLength: dp[n-1][k], substrings: result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP with a constrained path problem and reconstruction, a common real interview topic for large-scale environments like PayPal or Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP state: i, j, t, dir (0 right, 1 down)
- Turn counting via transition from one dir to the other
- Base cases for first row/column
- Path reconstruction pointers and memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) dp[0][j][0][0] = dp[0][j-1][0][0] + grid[0][j];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) dp[i][0][0][1] = dp[i-1][0][0][1] + grid[i][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromLeftSame = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromLeftTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromLeftSame, fromLeftTurn));
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromTopSame = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromTopTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromTopSame, fromTopTurn));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestDir = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
      if (dp[n-1][m-1][t][d] > best) {
        best = dp[n-1][m-1][t][d]; bestT = t; bestDir = d;
      }
    }
  }
  return {best, bestT, bestDir};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path coordinates efficiently?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n*m*k) or O(k) with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a fundamental DP pattern with an explicit gap constraint, a natural generalization of the classic non-adjacent selection.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index and remaining picks
- Handling fixed gap via i-g-1 transition
- Space- and time-complexity considerations
- Reconstruction of chosen indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumWithGap(A, k, g){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i-1
      if(t>0 && i-g-1>=0 && dp[i-g-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-g-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = i-g-1; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], indices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the exact indices efficiently?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k picks or variable gap?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

